    fix(board): repair event ids against output. Addresses DF-BOARDCTL-45.
    
    --repair-events minted runningMax+1 without ever looking at the ids a LATER
    event still carries: events 10, 9, 11 (file order) repaired to 10, 11, 11 —
    two events sharing id 11 — and the census still printed "duplicate ids: 0"
    because it counted the INPUT. Any consumer keyed on event id then sees an
    ambiguous record.
    
    The plan now treats "id == runningMax AND runningMax was minted by this pass"
    as an id to repair: a later event can only collide with a minted id when its
    own id equals the running max (anything larger lies above every id emitted so
    far), so 10, 9, 11 repairs to 10, 11, 12 — ascending, no shared ids — in one
    converging pass. Duplicates that already sit between two ORIGINAL ids are left
    untouched, so benign legacy duplicates are still not rewritten. The duplicate
    count is now a census of the repaired output, and the report line says so
    ("duplicate ids in repaired output: N").
    
    Regression coverage (internal/board/repair_events_test.go):
    - TestRepairEvents_neverCreatesDuplicateID applies the 10,9,11 fixture and
      asserts unique+ascending output ids (10, 11, 12), a 0 duplicate count,
      preserved non-id bytes (DF-BOARDCTL-44's splice contract), validate with no
      "duplicate event id" line, and a clean second pass.
    - TestRepairEvents_duplicateCensusCountsOutput pins the output census both
      ways: a clean board still reports its pre-existing duplicate (1), and a
      duplicate the repair renumbers away reports 0.
    Pre-fix both arm the bug: repaired ids = 10 11 11, duplicates = 1.
    
    Gates: gofmt clean; go build ./...; go vet ./...; go test ./... -count=1
    -short all ok. Live probe: boardctl normalize-board --repair-events --apply on
    ids 10,9,11 rewrote to 10,11,12 ("descending ids: 2", "duplicate ids in
    repaired output: 0", "applied") and `boardctl validate` then printed RESULT: OK
    with no duplicate event id warning (it FAILed before the repair).
    
